ed work has changed teaching has  changed critical research has continued  that includes research that's going on  still in our laboratories focused on  koba 19 some of which Nancy described to  you research to expand testing to  engineer increased ventilator capacity  to do clinical studies on drug  treatments that will lessen the symptoms  of this disease and of course the  frontline doctors nurses and patients  facing staff who are heroes in this  pandemic the question all of us want to  know is what happens next the way I  phrase it is what will I do on my second  one-hundred days in the job when will  laboratories be reactivated for  non-covered research when will the  library be of open again so that we can  do work in the social social sciences  and humanities when will we be able to  go back to work will the university be  open in the fall semester will we be  online or will we welcome students back  to the campus these continue to be  difficult questions and we do not yet  have final answers but we are taking  each of them systematically carefully  expeditiously with input from across the  campus to understand what the next what  the answers to those questions should be  last week President Salva announced a  process for addressing these important  questions that employs six faculty  committees seeking input from over 65  different faculty and additional senior  administrators who have experience from  across the university they will in turn  or our interns still seeking further  consultation from faculty students and  staff to identify what the answers to  these questions should be let me briefly  introduce you to those committees the  work that they're doing  and the way they are approaching their  mandate first and foremost we want to  follow the public health advice and so  the first committee is a public health  advisory committee that provides  guidance for the protocols and  procedures and directions we will follow  as we consider any other action that the  university will take the second is the  research continuity group recognizing  that it will be possible to reactivate  our laboratories and our libraries  before it will be possible to reactivate  the work that goes on in our classrooms  and dining halls this group is defining  how to do safe the labs in the libraries  with clear protocols and standards there  is also an academic continuity community  kanuha t group that is chaired by  pericles louis  pericles will be talking to us about the  details of his work in a minute  but I want to identify what that group  was charged to do one of the challenges  that I have in thinking about the  decision-making process in this is that  there all becomes a question of  probabilities of certain things falling  into line  will there be sufficient testing will  there be sufficient PPE and so to pull  apart that probability question and make  it possible to just clean think clearly  about the possibilities in front of us  we have asked one of the subcommittee's  under academic continuity to think about  the question what is the best version of  an online education that we can provide  recognizing that we now have several  months to prepare for it if that's what  we end up needing to do in the fall so  that's the thinking of one group there  is also a second group that is thinking  about the question what is the best  version of a safe residential in person  education that we can provide in the  fall recognizing that we will be under  constraints for public health  considerations those two considerations  those two groups thinking in parallel  with each other we think will be a defy  the best possible version of what  eventually becomes our decision for the  fall semester  there are also there's also a group  considering what are we doing in the  creative practice specifically around  the arts for example this represents  particularly new and different questions  different than what's being considered  in the other two groups how do you  social the distance of play or how do  you socially distance an orchestra so  what it means to bring the arts groups  reactivate the arts groups is an  important question all of this will be  informed by a group that's thinking  about operations how do we reimagine  work how do we reimagine teaching and  research and this group has  considerations from human resources  facilities IT and hospitality of course  we need to make an answer soon and the  sooner we can make an answer the better  it will be for our community as you plan  for what will happen in coming days  weeks and months we are committed to  making a we are committed to giving an  answer for what we'll have  in the fall semester no later than early  July so this will be approximately two  months from now as we giving us  approximately two months to then make  final plans for what the fall semester  will look like with those committees at  work I should say that they are working  incredibly hard they have made major  time commitments to get this work done  and I am very impressed with the  deliberations that they are already  making but of course we all want to know  the answer to the questions that they've  been tasked to answer Peter so I think  we should turn to Pericles and ask him  to tell us a little bit particularly  about the work of his committee and what  it is planning with respect to the  educational mission of the University  thank you Peter and Scott Yale of course  has been educating young people for  service for 319 years the educational  mission of the university is paramount  second only to the health and safety of  our staff our faculty and our students  and we need to be prepared to continue  this mission in the fall semester  like many peer colleges and universities  we're looking at two main scenarios for  planning purposes and we'll probably  wind up with a hybrid of the two if we  are unable to come together in person in  the fall we will provide remote or  online