oups  and we may have some hybrid of in-person  and online teaching it won't be exactly  the same in the graduate and  professional schools as it is in the  college we're looking at safe ways of  housing students including providing  facilities for quarantine and for self  isolation enabling contact tracing in  case there's a second wave of infection  and of course being prepared to treat  anyone who falls ill we plan to keep the  campus informed as as this discussion  proceeds and we'll provide more detail  in early summer I realize that it's  frustrating not to be able to give a  detailed account of each of these  scenarios at the moment but we actually  have we've been sort of trying to plan  out all the various options and  recognizing the complexity of the  problem and we want to present the  president and the provost with an  adequate map for making this decision  now Peter and Scott and I and all of our  faculty and staff are committed to  Yale's educational mission it's never  been more important than it is now  Yale has been through many crises in its  history  this is as far as I'm concerned this is  the biggest crisis I've ever seen and I  imagine it's the biggest crisis that  many of us have ever encountered but  we're a strong community and I think we  will come through this and ensure that  all Yale students get an excellent  education in the years to come so thank  you Peter and stop right thank you very  much Pericles Kim I want to throw this  back to you in your role as vice  president for university life what  concerns have your team been working on  in preparing for especially in this  context of so much uncertainty  so what I was saying was that one of the  things our staff hears a lot about are  people feeling a loss of people in place  because we're all scattered all over New  Haven all over the state many of us or  the country and the world and we miss  people talk about missing interacting  with their colleagues and their  classmates pursuing