th crisis Dean Brown mentioned one  of them Koba 19 is is affecting  different populations differently based  on race and socioeconomic status as she  mentioned including here in New Haven  we're conscious of that as it plays out  in the community the campus community as  well and of course there is news  nationally of the rise and racial  discrimination around Asia for against  Asians and Asian Americans and we want  people to know that we have resources  available to support people because we  really want to support those who are  facing harassment and discrimination so  we have a team of people around the  university who are working very hard to  make sure that the community is taken  care of and that we can connect with  each other I encourage you all all of us  to support each other as we go through  this process of trying to not only  continue to flatten a curve but also to  prepare to turn the corner that both you  and Scott have described great thank you  very much Kim Kim Nancy Scott pericles i  want to thank you all for what you're  doing for our community Kim I'd be  interested in hearing some of the  questions that you've been collecting  from participants so why don't we turn  to those questions now so a lot of  people that have submitted questions so  thank you everybody the first question  is for Nancy Nancy the question you're  being asked is when faculty students and  staff returned to campus what would we  do to reduce the possible transmission  of kovat 19 in terms of cleaning  social distancing PPE and the light  thank you for the year question yeah so  first I would say we will adhere to  current state and city guidelines at the  time that we reopen and these in turn  will depend on the prevalence of  infection at that time and so we may  need to make changes as the  characteristics of the epidemic and that  guidance changes I'd say social  distancing is the fundamental strategy  and it's going to include modifying our  physical spaces and our m